WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.631
I use so many agents at the same time that it's hard to keep up with all of them.

00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:06.633
So I gave them a voice and turned each one into my own Jarvis,

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:10.373
KC, two tests broke after your last change,

00:00:10.773 --> 00:00:14.100
but I already found the bug and left the fix ready for you to review.

00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:17.938
because now they call me when they finish, when they break and when they need me.

00:00:17.938 --> 00:00:21.800
And today I'll teach you how to do the same with your agent, be it Claude Code,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.471
Codex or any other one in the terminal.

00:00:23.471 --> 00:00:27.333
And the voice costs nothing, because Fish Audio released their API for free,

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:29.696
with no usage limit, until the end of November.

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:33.281
So let's go! If you use AI coding agents, you've surely run into this

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:36.764
problem. You give it a task, it starts working and you keep watching the terminal to see

00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:39.666
if it's done. After all, it can take a long time or be really fast.

00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:42.094
So sometimes you forget to hit "yes" on the screen,

00:00:42.094 --> 00:00:45.472
you have 5 terminals running at the same time and you don't notice that one has

00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:48.533
already finished or that one is waiting for your answer, for example.

00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:52.319
And I know it's a very specific problem, but it's still a problem that happens.

00:00:52.319 --> 00:00:56.280
And a lot of people have solutions for that, like, for example, adding a little sound,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.891
adding something specific, but that still doesn't help your productivity.

00:00:59.891 --> 00:01:02.512
So what I did, folks, was solve it in a different way.

00:01:02.512 --> 00:01:05.949
Instead of me watching them, they call me out loud when they need me.

00:01:05.949 --> 00:01:09.211
And I can be doing something else at the same time, I can be gaming,

00:01:09.211 --> 00:01:12.881
I can be working, studying, and they'll come talk to me when they need me.

00:01:12.881 --> 00:01:15.584
And to make this voice, I'll be using Fish Audio,

00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:17.668
which is the sponsor of this video.

00:01:17.668 --> 00:01:22.197
Fish Audio is an AI audio platform made for creators and devs, and they just

00:01:22.197 --> 00:01:26.869
released S2.1 Pro, their voice model, for free through the API and

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:29.672
with no usage limit until the end of November.

00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:34.272
And it's not a weaker version, it's the model from their paid plan, now for free.

00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:35.986
And this model does a lot of things.

00:01:35.986 --> 00:01:39.772
It speaks 83 languages in a single model, starts playing the audio almost instantly and

00:01:39.772 --> 00:01:42.119
understands emotion from the text itself.

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:45.159
So I can ask it to be excited, I can ask it to be slower, for example,

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:48.786
to sigh or whisper. Who knows, maybe even my voice here was made with Fish Audio, right?

00:01:48.786 --> 00:01:51.132
Which is exactly what I need in this video, okay?

00:01:51.132 --> 00:01:52.786
